WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.400
Tracking aim is uniquely interesting&nbsp;
in the sense that it appears to be much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.760
more difficult to do than it actually is.&nbsp;
The average player is far more capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.520
great tracking than they may realize.&nbsp;
It’s simply being inhibited by a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.240
barriers that can be overcome throughout&nbsp;
this video. And what’s important about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.200
that observation is that i’m referring to&nbsp;
aiming in-game, and not in aim trainers.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.000
You don’t need to be an aim training God like&nbsp;
Matty to have good tracking in Overwatch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.880
Battlefield, Apex and so on. But&nbsp;
do you need to be a Matty to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.960
great tracking in the hardest tracking&nbsp;
scenarios aim trainers have to offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.880
which place you in aiming situations that far&nbsp;
exceed the difficulty of anything you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.280
ever encounter in-game. There’s a key difference&nbsp;
there, and what we’re focusing on in this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:52.360
is having good tracking in FPS games with as&nbsp;
little time and effort invested as possible.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.560
You can always go the extra&nbsp;
mile afterwards, but at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.920
you may as well use the biggest and easiest&nbsp;
improvements to give you that solid foundation.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.280
And a quick disclaimer, as I mentioned&nbsp;
the point of this video is to improve your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.840
tracking as quickly and efficiently as possible,&nbsp;
so i’ll be condensing all of the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:16.080
and actionable information that I can into one&nbsp;
package. I want you to be able to finish this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.080
video and to know exactly what to implement&nbsp;
into your next games to instantly have better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.920
tracking. I cannot possibly cover every single&nbsp;
thing in one video so feel free to check out my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:32.480
past videos and stay tuned for future vids that&nbsp;
explain some of these concepts in further detail.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.680
Okay, firstly, tracking is primarily&nbsp;
about being able to move our crosshair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.960
at a consistent speed. Can we move&nbsp;
it slowly at one speed? Can we move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.960
it quickly at one speed? And so on. This&nbsp;
falls under the umbrella of smoothness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.400
but it’s not exactly the same thing so i’ll&nbsp;
avoid that terminology to avoid confusion.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.680
So long as your crosshair is over the target,&nbsp;
if it’s moving at the same speed as the target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.960
then you’re perfectly tracking them.&nbsp;
The speed that you perceive them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.920
to be moving at comes down to your distance&nbsp;
to the target, the direction they’re moving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:10.480
whether they’re adsing, crouching and so on.&nbsp;
All of these little variables will change how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.080
fast you need to move your crosshair, but&nbsp;
the good news is that each encounter will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.560
mostly stick to one speed. If you can&nbsp;
match that speed, you can track them.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.520
For example, With a mouse script that moves&nbsp;
at a uniform speed, you can see it tracking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:30.320
this mid ranged target. But then if we move&nbsp;
closer, it’s no longer moving quickly enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.520
And if we move further away, now it’s moving too&nbsp;
quickly. I can change the speed of the script to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.200
suit each situation, and this is essentially what&nbsp;
we need to be doing ourselves. We need to quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.920
determine how fast an enemy is moving, and prepare&nbsp;
ourselves to track at that particular speed.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.800
I should clarify that there can be some&nbsp;
deviation in this perceived target speed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.440
For example if a target is running toward&nbsp;
you, you barely have to move at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.160
but when they go past you, you&nbsp;
suddenly have to aim very quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.200
before hardly needing to move once again. The&nbsp;
vast majority of aiming encounters are barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.680
impacted by this deviation in speed,&nbsp;
so for the most-part we can ignore it.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.440
What this means is that you should be approaching&nbsp;
each fight with an idea of what hand and mouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.560
movements are going to be needed. If you’re&nbsp;
about to track someone in the distance, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.840
need to be ready for some very slow and deliberate&nbsp;
movements. If a target jumps out in front of you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.920
it’s time to use larger movements. This&nbsp;
isn’t permission to panic and swipe your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.440
mouse around by the way, larger movements&nbsp;
still need consistant speed and control.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.600
Okay, so we know that moving the crosshair&nbsp;
at a consistent speed is important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.640
but if you aren’t able to move&nbsp;
it at a consistent speed then it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.200
won’t help you out very much. So let’s&nbsp;
address any barriers before moving on.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.680
If your crosshair is constantly jumping&nbsp;
in front and behind your target the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.120
culprit is typically mouse sensitivity&nbsp;
being too fast. For most modern FPS games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:01.680
a sensitivity between 25 and 50cm per 360 is&nbsp;
generally pretty safe. It will depend on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.400
game you are playing of course. In tac shooters&nbsp;
like Valorant and Counter Strike, it looks more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:14.280
like 35-80cm. You can use sites like Aiming.pro&nbsp;
to check what cm/360 you are currently playing on.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.720
If you are playing on default settings&nbsp;
at something like 5cm/360, you will never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.960
have great aim, it doesn’t matter if&nbsp;
it feels comfortable to you right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:29.240
you are at a massive mechanical disadvantage&nbsp;
that is basically impossible to overcome.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.560
The next thing you can check or fix is&nbsp;
your friction against the mousepad and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:39.160
desk. If you are in a humid environment&nbsp;
and your skin sticks and rolls rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:44.080
gliding along the surface you can see this&nbsp;
jittery aim as a result. Regularly washing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.520
your hands and any skin that contacts the&nbsp;
desk with soap and water while playing can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.400
help reset this clamminess and provide a&nbsp;
better glide for awhile. Wearing a sleeve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.920
is also a great option, especially to&nbsp;
have consistent friction day to day.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.520
There are plenty of sleeve options out there&nbsp;
that should do the trick if you’re having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:07.160
issues. I’ve tried over 20 sleeves by now and&nbsp;
just had my own custom sleeves designed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:11.760
Scody who are a premium sportswear brand&nbsp;
here in Australia. You can find them at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.960
Struthgaminggear.com. I’ll talk more about their&nbsp;
design and what they do in another video though.&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.080
What happens if we are tracking with&nbsp;
the wrong crosshair speed? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:27.240
the big thing to avoid is overreactive aim.&nbsp;
Say you’re aiming too fast and move ahead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:34.160
suddenly you flick back and aim way too slowly and&nbsp;
need to flick forward again, and so on. Unless you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.560
catastrophically misjudge the speed, you’ll only&nbsp;
ever need slight adjustments. To make this easier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.880
really think about how your hand is moving at&nbsp;
the time. Depending on whether you’re too slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:53.120
or too fast, gently dial up or dial back that&nbsp;
hand speed. A small change goes a long way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.640
so keep things gradual and controlled,&nbsp;
instead of overreactive and exaggerated.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.600
The final, quick solution to this jittery aim&nbsp;
comes down to reading movement and avoiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:09.640
constantly triggering your reaction time tax.&nbsp;
This is it’s own topic, so if your jitteriness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:14.560
hasn’t been fixed yet, don’t stress, the next&nbsp;
chapter will cover this bit in more detail.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.360
To tie this concept of consistant speed&nbsp;
and smoothness together, it’s time to dive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.240
into the most important skill required for&nbsp;
great tracking, which is movement reading.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.240
Fortunately, for how important this&nbsp;
is, it’s also the easiest to implement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.200
into your gameplay. No need for hundreds of&nbsp;
hours of aim training or anything like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.640
a simple mental reminder is all it takes&nbsp;
for you to use this in your next game.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:42.440
The key here is to acknowledge the goals&nbsp;
of the player or NPC you are aiming at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:47.560
and the environment you are in. It’s important to&nbsp;
remember that there is another person just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:52.920
you piloting that enemy on your screen,&nbsp;
or at least some logic if it’s an NPC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:57.760
This means they will have similar goals and&nbsp;
instincts to you that you need to be aware of.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.320
I really like this clip from Darktide as&nbsp;
an example of reading movement. I know this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:08.480
charging NPC wants to reach me or my team, and to&nbsp;
do so they’ll have to run up these stairs. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.920
means I know exactly where it’s going and can plan&nbsp;
out my tracking movements ahead of time. I track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.360
it running down and smoothly follow its change&nbsp;
in direction up the stairs. If I wasn’t reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.840
movement and the constraints of the environment&nbsp;
around me (which in this case, are the stairs it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.720
needs to climb), then this change in direction&nbsp;
would come as a surprise and I would have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:35.040
wait 200ish milliseconds for my reaction time&nbsp;
to catch up before adjusting back to the enemy.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.320
In this example, I start shooting at this&nbsp;
Bloodhound. Within the first few shots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:44.320
it becomes clear that they aren’t willing to&nbsp;
take this bad fight as they don’t look my way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:49.480
so it becomes clear that this player’s current&nbsp;
goal is to get to the nearest cover over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:54.760
This means I can plan ahead a simple one&nbsp;
directional track and land some easy shots.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:59.640
And here, this enemy in front of me jumps, and&nbsp;
we can see their knee shoot out to our left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:04.440
so we know they’re going to be falling in&nbsp;
a predictable arc down here. And similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.800
to our Darktide example, this player is&nbsp;
running up the stairs to reach the roof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.920
so we can plan our tracking ahead of time to&nbsp;
follow the path they will most likely take.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.440
Knowing where an enemy is moving makes tracking&nbsp;
significantly easier to do. I think a problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:25.400
many players run into with tracking and aiming&nbsp;
in general, is overestimating the evasiveness and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:30.640
randomness of enemies on their screen. They treat&nbsp;
these enemies as if they can move in impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:36.840
ways and have overreactive, jittery and chaotic&nbsp;
looking aim as a result. Their brain is constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.040
paying the reaction time tax and playing&nbsp;
catchup to every little movement and action.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:45.800
It’s a weird way to think about it,&nbsp;
but you can essentially consider that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.600
everything you are seeing in-game is&nbsp;
200~ish milliseconds in the past. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:55.720
you’re playing FPS games that inherently&nbsp;
require precise inputs, this delay can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.360
very punishing if you’re in a constant cycle&nbsp;
of reacting to what’s happening on screen.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:05.640
This is why predicting movement and planning&nbsp;
aiming movements in advance is so important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:10.960
With the correct read, you can circumvent the&nbsp;
reaction time tax, plan aiming movements that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:16.520
may span seconds rather than milliseconds and&nbsp;
ultimately aim at your enemies in real-time.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:21.080
To hammer this point home, I have two examples&nbsp;
from aim training that reflect this principle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:25.440
very clearly. First, in a KovaaK’s&nbsp;
scenario like Smoothness Training 60,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.320
the target follows a pre-determined path.&nbsp;
Knowing exactly where the target is moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:34.280
means you can predict and lock in your&nbsp;
mouse movements for very easy tracking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:38.000
and even have perfect accuracy&nbsp;
through the changes in directions.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.920
But if I jump into Mid-range Strafes Invincible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.600
I can still track it during the strafe, but&nbsp;
every change in direction incurs a reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:51.120
time tax that I cannot avoid. The strafe&nbsp;
intervals are random so I can’t reliably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.440
predict them. Even though both examples have&nbsp;
easy to track targets that change directions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:01.720
the one with predictable movement is easier&nbsp;
to aim at than the one with random movement.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:07.160
And the second example is with scenarios&nbsp;
like 1wall6targets TE. To score highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.360
in these scenarios, you need to employ a&nbsp;
skill called routing. Which is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:17.080
planning your path and subsequently your mouse&nbsp;
movements ahead of time. When played poorly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:21.240
the player is often in a cycle of&nbsp;
reacting after every shot. You hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.680
a target &gt; choose the next target &gt; shoot&nbsp;
it &gt; choose the next target, shoot it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.400
and so on. But each time you choose a new&nbsp;
target, you’re incurring that reaction time tax.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:35.360
Whereas if you plan your shots ahead&nbsp;
of time you can instantly move on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:40.520
saving 200ish milliseconds in reaction&nbsp;
time, and even more by optimizing mouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:44.720
movements instead of constantly stopping and&nbsp;
starting. There is more to routing than that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.840
for scoring purposes, but for the principle&nbsp;
of movement reading and the importance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.120
planning mouse movements ahead of time,&nbsp;
this was the important part to cover.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:57.240
Okay, so how do you actually read&nbsp;
movement? Almost all of this skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:01.360
comes immediately from focusing on&nbsp;
your target, not just looking at it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:06.240
there is a big difference. I have a video all&nbsp;
about it linked below if you are unfamiliar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.920
with it. The quick version is that you can look&nbsp;
at this fan and the blades spin around in a blur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:16.120
but if you focus on a single blade it&nbsp;
comes into focus and you can track it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:21.760
spinning around. That shift in perspective is&nbsp;
the difference between looking, and focusing.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.480
By focusing on your target you absorb&nbsp;
their visual cues. How fast they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:29.520
moving, what direction they’re&nbsp;
moving, where they’re looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:33.560
their legs just pointed a different&nbsp;
direction so they’re changing direction etc.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.280
The rest of the skill comes down to&nbsp;
game sense and consideration of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:43.240
surrounding environment and enemy goals.&nbsp;
If you shoot an enemy that’s in the open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:48.080
they’re likely going to b-line for the nearest&nbsp;
cover so you can plan your aiming movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:52.960
accordingly. If you’re fighting an enemy in&nbsp;
a tight corridor and they’re AD strafing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:58.520
you know they can’t strafe any further than the&nbsp;
wall and can predictively track them accordingly.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:03.200
If an enemy jumps you know they’re mostly&nbsp;
locked into a certain trajectory. If they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:07.040
jump off a building or use a&nbsp;
zipline, jumppad and so on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:11.840
the same applies. You can almost imagine&nbsp;
this predictive line forming whenever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.320
you see an enemy, and all you need to&nbsp;
do is paint over it with your mouse.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.520
This can be tricker to learn as it’s&nbsp;
more of a conceptual understanding and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:25.240
doesn’t always follow strict rules but I&nbsp;
honestly think the majority of players are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:30.080
much better at this than they would think,&nbsp;
they simply need to remember to consider it.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:35.320
For me, when i’m about to track someone I&nbsp;
quickly assess their distance and speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:39.600
acknowledge the environment and how it may&nbsp;
influence the fight and prepare the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.120
mouse movements. This doesn’t require&nbsp;
you to stop and literally think these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:48.880
steps through before shooting, it’s simpler&nbsp;
than it appears so try not to overthink it.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:52.040
The enemy distance is automatic to interpret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:57.000
we instinctively know if something is far&nbsp;
away or not, it doesn’t require effort to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:02.320
think about. The speed is simple too.&nbsp;
Are they running, walking, crouching?

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:07.880
Environment can be a little trickier but still&nbsp;
very manageable. All you’re really looking for is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:13.320
what cover is around them. That can be a rock&nbsp;
in a field, or the walls inside a building.

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:17.280
The trick is to acknowledge these&nbsp;
variables and plan accordingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:20.360
rather than autopiloting. And to be&nbsp;
fair, the better you get at aiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:24.680
and playing FPS games in general,&nbsp;
the more automatic this becomes.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:30.440
Movement is a massive topic, so I'll just cover&nbsp;
the essentials for tracking. Our own movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:35.480
is almost like having another mouse plugged in&nbsp;
that we’re aiming with. It’s another way to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:41.040
our crosshair around, we just have to make sure&nbsp;
it’s helping us to aim, and not throwing us off.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.800
If you have played on a fast sensitivity before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:47.680
you may have noticed that you often use&nbsp;
your movement to make small crosshair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:51.480
adjustments rather than the mouse.&nbsp;
This doesn’t really mean anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:56.840
it just goes to show that you essentially have&nbsp;
two different inputs that control your crosshair.

00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:03.000
For tracking, we can think of it as a sensitivity&nbsp;
multiplier. Using the same script from before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:08.120
Aiming to the right ends up here. Yet if&nbsp;
we move to the right at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:13.440
we end up further out. And if we move&nbsp;
to the left, we fall much shorter. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:18.480
shows us that moving while aiming can&nbsp;
dampen or amplify our mouse inputs.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.600
This can be quite jarring if we change directions&nbsp;
while tracking something. A simple rule to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:30.040
in-game that works in almost any situation is&nbsp;
to combine your changes in movement direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:35.800
and crosshair speed and/or direction. This&nbsp;
falls under something called anti-mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:41.040
strafing. Where if your opponent is moving&nbsp;
left, you move to the right, and vice versa.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.400
Because you are moving your crosshair in the&nbsp;
opposite direction to your movement you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:55.200
to aim more, this isn’t a bad thing,&nbsp;
just something to keep in mind. Modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:00.000
games like Apex Legends and The Finals&nbsp;
use a mechanic called recoil smoothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.440
where if your crosshair is moving&nbsp;
faster than a certain threshold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.320
your recoil is greatly reduced. Anti mirror&nbsp;
strafing helps to engage this mechanic more often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:13.200
which is another good reason for using it.&nbsp;
Although I would like to emphasize that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:18.080
don’t need to restrict your movements to only&nbsp;
copy the enemy. The important part to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:24.040
away from this is to associate your changes in&nbsp;
movement with changes to your crosshair behavior.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:29.600
The alternative is mirror strafing, where by&nbsp;
copying the enemy’s movement you don’t have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:35.080
aim at all. This technique makes aiming very easy,&nbsp;
as you barely need to move your mouse at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:40.440
if any. The problem is that it can be difficult&nbsp;
to properly sync up, especially if the target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:45.040
is facing and fighting you. It’s still worth&nbsp;
looking out for moments that you can use this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:50.320
as it’s essentially free tracking. This clip&nbsp;
ties in all three concepts of acknowledging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.760
movement’s impact on crosshair speed, mirror&nbsp;
strafing, and anti mirror strafing. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.760 --> 00:16:00.040
also showing off some movement reading&nbsp;
and assessing the environment around me.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:05.480
I see this enemy running to my right and they’re&nbsp;
not looking at me. This means I can mirror strafe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:10.520
for free shots and barely have to move my&nbsp;
crosshair at all. Mirror strafing here also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:15.800
means I can move off this bridge that’s out in&nbsp;
the open and move closer to cover. I have to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:21.800
a very slight crosshair adjustment because i’m&nbsp;
slowed down by ADSing but it’s hardly noticeable.

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:26.440
This Bloodhound starts shooting at me so I can&nbsp;
no longer hold my current strafe without getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:31.800
beamed. I strafe the other way to avoid their&nbsp;
shots, but notice how my crosshair speed stays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:37.480
the exact same? It should have slowed down when&nbsp;
I changed direction, but because I associate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:43.240
movement changes with changes to crosshair speed,&nbsp;
when I hit A on my keyboard to move the other way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:47.520
I knew that was the time to move my&nbsp;
mouse slightly faster to compensate.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:50.920
I switch over to the Bloodhound&nbsp;
and start anti mirror strafing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:56.280
so I am moving to the right and aiming to the&nbsp;
left. Notice how i’m now playing from cover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.280 --> 00:17:00.560
which is a result of all of the movement&nbsp;
used so far to both help me aim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:04.600
and to position myself more safely&nbsp;
than standing out on that bridge.

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:09.800
So, to sum up our movement, we can think of&nbsp;
it as having a second mouse plugged in. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:15.320
don’t respect that, then it becomes a conflicting&nbsp;
input that throws us off. But if we acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:20.120
that it’s there, and know what impact it has&nbsp;
on our aim, we can use it to our advantage.

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:24.000
Aiming at targets that quickly change&nbsp;
direction, known as AD strafing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:28.160
is the hardest part of tracking but&nbsp;
it’s nothing we can’t overcome. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:33.080
is a big topic that deserves it’s own video,&nbsp;
so once again i’ll just cover the main points.

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:37.480
On the surface AD strafing can seem&nbsp;
impossibly fast and unpredictable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:42.480
aim at, but in reality it is far more&nbsp;
standardized than you may realize.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:47.760
The main problem to avoid is succumbing to that&nbsp;
reaction time tax, where you’re in this constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:52.880
cycle of reacting to their change in direction,&nbsp;
flicking back to them, then they change direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:58.360
again, your reaction time catches up, you flick&nbsp;
back and so on. Resulting in an aiming pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:02.680
that looks like you’re just flicking back&nbsp;
and forth and not actually tracking them.

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:06.960
What we’re mainly looking for instead&nbsp;
is to have a smoother transition between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.600
directions. You will likely still need&nbsp;
a little flick to get back to target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:16.200
but you want to ensure you are actively&nbsp;
tracking in-between. Your mouse should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:20.200
feel like it’s constantly in movement,&nbsp;
rather than stopping and starting.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:24.680
If you have KovaaK’s you can drill this in&nbsp;
some scenarios I whipped up a while back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:28.800
These were never published, they’re not&nbsp;
tuned for scoring or anything like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:33.520
I made them purely to work on having smoother&nbsp;
AD tracking. I have just uploaded them as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:37.600
they’re now relevant for this video so I’ll&nbsp;
link to the playlist in the description.

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:41.720
When it comes to the AD strafing&nbsp;
in-game, it is far less random than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:48.040
it may seem. Intentionally randomizing your&nbsp;
AD strafe is actually quite difficult to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:52.200
particularly in the chaos of a fight.&nbsp;
You can try it yourself right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:57.200
try to alternate hitting A and D in a&nbsp;
weird, random sequence. And remember if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:01.960
you move between them too fast you will&nbsp;
look like you’re standing still in-game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:06.880
so you still need to try and hold at least some&nbsp;
of the presses to have a useable AD strafe.

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:10.840
Because of this, almost everyone you&nbsp;
encounter in-game will alternate in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:15.840
reliable pattern. And probably in a very&nbsp;
similar cadence to your own strafing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.800
so you can use your own pattern as a reference.

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:23.760
So, this leaves us with the choice between&nbsp;
reacting to AD strafing. Where we wait for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:28.360
visual confirmation that they have changed&nbsp;
direction, and do our best to quickly flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:33.520
back to them and transition into a smooth&nbsp;
track. Or predicting when they will change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:39.240
direction and smoothly changing our crosshair&nbsp;
direction before we have visual confirmation.

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:44.440
Generally speaking, reactive aim is safer&nbsp;
and more consistent, so it’s a good option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:50.080
when you are ahead in a fight and can accept&nbsp;
a few missed shots when they change direction.

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:54.840
Overall, predicting their strafes has&nbsp;
the highest potential damage output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:59.440
but you are at risk of throwing off your own&nbsp;
aim if they don’t strafe within that window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:05.880
of time. In my opinion, predicting is the superior&nbsp;
option and is a skill that is well worth learning.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:11.040
But in the big picture, you’ll want to be&nbsp;
utilizing both depending on the situation.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:15.800
To accurately predict, and to know when to&nbsp;
predict, we have to consider the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:21.280
and environment. In this example, I catch this&nbsp;
Wattson jumping off and by the time she lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:26.320
i’ve already landed a lot of damage. This means&nbsp;
that player is very likely thinking they need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:32.600
to stop taking damage asap, so there’s almost no&nbsp;
chance this player would keep moving to my left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:36.880
(which is the same direction they’ve been&nbsp;
taking damage in) so I can start moving my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:42.320
crosshair to the right, well before my reaction&nbsp;
time can acknowledge they changed direction.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:47.760
And on the flip side of this, here I am at very&nbsp;
low health trying to heal and I see someone shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:53.400
past me so I know I have to commit to a heavily&nbsp;
disadvantaged fight. If I have any chance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:59.000
winning this, I need to land every shot I can,&nbsp;
so predicting is the only way I can come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:04.720
enough damage to turn the tide. Sure, they should&nbsp;
have killed me, and if I played purely reactively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:08.680
they almost certainly would have had&nbsp;
enough time to land that final hit.

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:14.480
To sum up tracking AD strafing targets, you can&nbsp;
think of like you’re aiming at yourself. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:20.800
imagine that player in front of you, is actually&nbsp;
you, and not some unpredictable random being. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:26.520
their situation, what would you do? Most of the&nbsp;
time in this context that will mean AD strafing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:32.480
at a regular cadence to avoid damage (because&nbsp;
being intentionally random is difficult),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:37.080
and preferring to move toward cover,&nbsp;
especially if you’re taking damage.

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:42.160
And finally, let’s quickly cover the concept&nbsp;
of minimizing your mouse movements. Once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:48.080
again this topic deserves it’s own video so i’ll&nbsp;
condense it down into juicy, actionable pieces.

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:53.680
Your targets are bound by the rules and mechanics&nbsp;
of that game. They aren’t going to zip around your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:59.800
screen randomly, so your crosshair and mouse also&nbsp;
should not be zipping around all over the place.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:02.520
If you’re the type of player&nbsp;
who panics during fights and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:06.320
whips their crosshair around, this&nbsp;
can make a huge difference for you.

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:10.600
The concept is very simple, and it all&nbsp;
comes down to aiming less. Whether a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:15.440
target is far away or right in your face,&nbsp;
moving your crosshair around less will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:20.320
almost always be the right play. You will&nbsp;
be astonished at how many more one clips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:25.680
and big chunks of damage you can get simply&nbsp;
from not aiming once you’re already on target.

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:28.720
You may have noticed this yourself&nbsp;
too if you have ever suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:32.520
switched to playing on very low&nbsp;
sensitivity. Your aim in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:37.720
might suck or feel restricted, but you may&nbsp;
notice these random one clips that occur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:42.640
primarily because if your crosshair is on&nbsp;
target, it’s hard to move it away from it.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:46.520
To get in the zone and reduce wasted&nbsp;
crosshair movement, something I like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:51.840
to do is focus on my crosshair placement,&nbsp;
ensuring it’s at chest or head height and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:56.800
pointing toward where enemies are most likely&nbsp;
to be. And also moving it around smoothly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:01.280
rather than flicking to different points&nbsp;
of interest. This isn’t necessarily better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:06.160
but it gets you in the right mindset of&nbsp;
smoothly aiming and slowing things down.

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:10.240
During a fight you will still want to&nbsp;
flick to a target if it’s necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:15.120
this is more about navigating the environment&nbsp;
and priming yourself for controlled aim once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:18.960
a fight starts. Another helpful way&nbsp;
to think of it would be like trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:24.080
to replicate what controller aim looks&nbsp;
like. The speed limits of an analog stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:29.560
and the stickiness of aim assist results in&nbsp;
slow, deliberate and efficient looking aim.

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:34.840
Okay, so we’ve covered a lot of information so&nbsp;
far, to help summarize things i’ve added chapters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:41.120
to this video so you can easily jump around to&nbsp;
each topic as needed. Now, let’s quickly recap.

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:45.920
Consistent Speed: If you can move your&nbsp;
crosshair at the same speed as your target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:51.560
you can track them. The perceived speed of the&nbsp;
target will change depending on their distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:58.440
and what they’re doing. So quickly assess the&nbsp;
situation and prepare the right mouse movements.

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:04.280
Movement reading: Focus on your target, don’t&nbsp;
just look at them. Absorb their visual cues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:08.520
and think about the enemy’s goals and&nbsp;
motivations. Create these predictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:12.120
paths in your mind that you’ll&nbsp;
paint over with your crosshair.

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:17.880
Movement: Your own movement is like having a&nbsp;
second mouse plugged in, so be aware of how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:24.520
will affect your aim. Remember to sync up changes&nbsp;
in movement, with changes in your mouse movement.

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:28.560
AD Strafing: AD strafing is very predictable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:33.800
consistent. When you know the enemy&nbsp;
wants to or needs to start AD strafing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:39.200
you can start tracking back and forth with&nbsp;
smooth transitions at a regular cadence.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:46.160
Minimizing Mouse Movement: Try to reduce wasted&nbsp;
mouse movements and over-reactive aiming. Slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:52.360
things down and play more smoothly. Mimicking&nbsp;
controller aim can be a handy cue to use.

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:57.000
Tracking is a skill that draws from just&nbsp;
about every possible discipline, skill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:01.840
technique and so on within aiming. Despite&nbsp;
this, I would argue that good tracking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:07.480
FPS games requires very little mouse control&nbsp;
and physical ability. The skill really comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:11.520
down to these auxiliary concepts&nbsp;
like movement reading and settings.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:16.600
Tracking weapons in general are typically&nbsp;
quite forgiving too. You have large magazines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:19.360
and whether you hit the edge of&nbsp;
a target, or the center doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:22.840
really matter. If you’re aiming in the&nbsp;
center and hit them right on the edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:27.200
who cares? You technically missed your&nbsp;
target but still get a hit anyway.

00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:32.160
I’m not trying to downplay the skillcap of&nbsp;
tracking, the skillcap is absurdly high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:38.440
it’s just that that level of mastery isn’t really&nbsp;
needed to track well in any popular FPS games.

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:42.320
Anecdotally, I would consider myself&nbsp;
to be a flick shot specialist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:45.400
and while i’ve not pushed for aim&nbsp;
training scores for many years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:50.840
i’ve always been significantly better at&nbsp;
clicking scenarios than tracking. Yet in-game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:55.440
my tracking has always been just as good, if&nbsp;
not better, and it all comes down to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.440 --> 00:26:00.440
other skills like movement reading. By applying&nbsp;
them, you can make tracking so much easier on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:05.800
yourself. You can go a long way with tracking&nbsp;
in-game, without needing insane mouse control.

